1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,892
This is MATH 239, video 18 b. We solve questions 6 to 10 of the Practice Midterm. Video 18 a did questions 1 to 5.

2
00:00:10,342 --> 00:00:26,363
Question 6 is about binary strings. Question 7 asks for one coefficient. Question 8 is about a product of series. Question 9 is about palindromes. Question 10 is about recurrences. For each question, pause the video and try it first.

3
00:00:26,813 --> 00:00:36,984
Question 6 a. Here is an expression for a set of binary strings. Is it ambiguous or unambiguous? We must prove the answer. Pause the video and try it.

4
00:00:37,434 --> 00:00:47,511
Read the expression in three parts. The first part, 000 star, makes a block of 0s. Its length is a multiple of 3.

5
00:00:47,961 --> 00:01:00,872
The middle part repeats a piece. Each piece is 111, then pairs 11, so a block of 1s of odd length, at least 3. Then it has 0s in groups of four, possibly none.

6
00:01:01,322 --> 00:01:12,235
The last part is epsilon, or 111 star. 111 star is 11 followed by 1 star. So it is a block of 1s of length at least 2.

7
00:01:12,685 --> 00:01:23,505
The middle part and the last part can both make a block of 1s. So one block of 1s can come from two places. An expression is ambiguous when some string is produced in two or more ways.

8
00:01:23,955 --> 00:01:26,277
Take the short string 111.

9
00:01:26,727 --> 00:01:38,523
Way 1. The first part gives epsilon. The middle part makes one pass: the piece 111, with no extra pairs and no 0s. The last part gives epsilon.

10
00:01:38,973 --> 00:01:47,147
Way 2. The first part gives epsilon. The middle part makes zero passes. The last part, 111 star, gives 111.

11
00:01:47,597 --> 00:01:54,191
These are two different productions of the same string. So the expression is ambiguous. One example is enough for the proof.

12
00:01:54,641 --> 00:02:03,558
Longer strings of 1s have even more productions. For 11111, way 1 is one pass of length 5.

13
00:02:04,008 --> 00:02:07,537
Way 2 is a pass 111, then the end gives 11.

14
00:02:07,987 --> 00:02:24,520
Way 3 is zero passes, and the end gives all five 1s. So 11111 has three productions. A computer count agrees: 111 has two, and 11111 has three.

15
00:02:24,970 --> 00:02:32,261
Here is the written answer. Say ambiguous. Give one string, and show two different productions of it. This is the full proof.

16
00:02:32,711 --> 00:02:46,689
A common mistake: each part is unambiguous, so the student says the whole is unambiguous. This is not enough. By Lemma 3.9, each concatenation must also split in only one way. Here it does not.

17
00:02:47,139 --> 00:02:56,334
Question 6 b. We are told that this expression is unambiguous. Find the generating series of its strings, with length as the weight. Pause the video and try it.

18
00:02:56,784 --> 00:03:18,054
Piece A is 011, then 111 star. Its strings are one 0, then a block of 1s of length 2, 5, 8, and so on. 011 gives x cubed. 111 star gives one over one minus x cubed. So piece A gives x cubed over one minus x cubed.

19
00:03:18,504 --> 00:03:38,659
Piece B is 000, then 0 star, then 1, then 1 star. So it is a block of at least three 0s, then a block of at least one 1. It gives x cubed, times one over one minus x, times x, times one over one minus x. That is x to the 4 over the square of one minus x.

20
00:03:39,109 --> 00:03:46,400
The end is epsilon, 0, or 00. It gives 1 plus x plus x squared.

21
00:03:46,850 --> 00:03:49,961
Now combine the parts. The two pieces are on the left.

22
00:03:50,411 --> 00:04:04,993
The piece is A or B. By the Sum Lemma, one piece gives A of x plus B of x. The union is disjoint. A piece of type A starts with one 0 and then a 1. A piece of type B starts with three 0s.

23
00:04:05,443 --> 00:04:11,898
The pieces repeat with a star. By the String Lemma, the repetition gives one over one minus A minus B.

24
00:04:12,348 --> 00:04:17,875
Then the end follows. By the Product Lemma, multiply by 1 plus x plus x squared.

25
00:04:18,325 --> 00:04:38,805
So phi of x is a fraction. The top is 1 plus x plus x squared. The bottom is one, minus x cubed over one minus x cubed, minus x to the 4 over the square of one minus x. The expression is unambiguous, so by Theorem 3.13 this is the generating series. The question does not ask us to simplify it.

26
00:04:39,255 --> 00:04:46,360
Expand the series. The coefficients start 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 4, 8, 13.

27
00:04:46,810 --> 00:04:57,398
Length 4 must have 2 strings. They are 0001, which is one piece of type B, and 0110, which is piece A, then the end 0.

28
00:04:57,848 --> 00:05:10,573
Length 5 must have 4 strings. They are 00001, 00010, 00011, and 01100. The coefficients agree.

29
00:05:11,023 --> 00:05:18,082
A computer listed all strings up to length 12. Each one is produced at most once. The counts match the series for each length.

30
00:05:18,532 --> 00:05:29,816
Question 6 c. We need an unambiguous expression for the strings where each block of 1s of even length is followed by a block of 0s of length at least 3. Pause the video and try it.

31
00:05:30,266 --> 00:05:40,668
Test some strings. In 0110001, the block 11 is even. The next block is 000, of length 3. So the string is in the set.

32
00:05:41,118 --> 00:05:48,781
In 110, the block 11 is even, but the next 0 block has length 1. So 110 is not in the set.

33
00:05:49,231 --> 00:05:58,565
In 0111, the only 1 block is 111. It is odd, so there is no condition. 0111 is in the set.

34
00:05:59,015 --> 00:06:06,214
In the string 11, the even block is at the end. No block of 0s follows it. So 11 is not in the set.

35
00:06:06,664 --> 00:06:23,846
Start from the block decomposition, 0 star, then 1 1 star 0 0 star, repeated, then 1 star. It produces each binary string once, block by block. Each pass of the middle part is a block of 1s, then the block of 0s after it.

36
00:06:24,296 --> 00:06:34,374
Split each pass by the length of its 1 block. If the 1 block is odd, write it as 1, then 11 star. Any 0 block of length at least 1 can follow.

37
00:06:34,824 --> 00:06:46,016
If the 1 block is even, write it as 11, then 11 star. Then the 0 block must have length at least 3: 000, then 0 star.

38
00:06:46,466 --> 00:06:56,172
The last part, 1 star, is a block of 1s with nothing after it. So it must be empty or odd: epsilon, or 1 then 11 star.

39
00:06:56,622 --> 00:06:59,594
Put the parts together. This is the expression for the set.

40
00:07:00,044 --> 00:07:01,391
Here is the expression.

41
00:07:01,841 --> 00:07:09,271
The written answer has short explanations. Step 1. Start from the block decomposition. It is unambiguous.

42
00:07:09,721 --> 00:07:24,768
Step 2. Each pass is a 1 block and the 0 block after it. An odd 1 block can have any 0 block after it. An even 1 block needs a 0 block of length at least 3. The two cases are disjoint, because the parity of the 1 block differs.

43
00:07:25,218 --> 00:07:32,509
Step 3. The last block of 1s has no 0 block after it. So it must be odd, or not there.

44
00:07:32,959 --> 00:07:40,017
Step 4. We only restricted the parts of an unambiguous expression. So the new expression is unambiguous.

45
00:07:40,467 --> 00:08:03,177
Examples. 011000101 splits into 0, then 11 with 000, then 1 with 0, then the last block 1. The string 1100011 ends in an even block, so the expression does not produce it. A computer check of all strings up to length 11 agrees with the condition.

46
00:08:03,627 --> 00:08:15,794
Question 7. Find the coefficient of x to the 70 in x cubed over one minus 4 x to the 30, to the power 20, times one minus x, cubed. Pause the video and try it.

47
00:08:16,244 --> 00:08:27,993
First remove the x cubed. Multiplying by x cubed shifts every coefficient 3 places to the right. So we need the coefficient of x to the 67 in F of x, the product of the two inverse powers.

48
00:08:28,443 --> 00:08:38,846
Each factor is a binomial series. One over one minus y, to the power t, is the sum over j of j plus t minus 1 choose t minus 1, times y to the j.

49
00:08:39,296 --> 00:08:51,927
Expand the first factor with y equal to 4 x to the 30 and t equal to 20. The term for j is j plus 19 choose 19, times 4 to the j, times x to the 30 j.

50
00:08:52,377 --> 00:09:05,241
The product formula pairs a power x to the 30 j from the first factor with x to the m from the second. We need 30 j plus m equal to 67. j equal to 0 gives m equal to 67.

51
00:09:05,691 --> 00:09:08,989
j equal to 1 gives m equal to 37.

52
00:09:09,439 --> 00:09:12,179
j equal to 2 gives m equal to 7.

53
00:09:12,629 --> 00:09:18,155
j equal to 3 needs 90, which is more than 67. So there are exactly three terms.

54
00:09:18,605 --> 00:09:25,246
j equal to 0. The first factor gives 1. The second gives 69 choose 2, which is 2346.

55
00:09:25,696 --> 00:09:39,953
j equal to 1. The first factor gives 20 choose 19, times 4, which is 80. The second gives 39 choose 2, which is 741. The product is 59280.

56
00:09:40,403 --> 00:09:56,750
j equal to 2. The first factor gives 21 choose 19, times 16, which is 3360. The second gives 9 choose 2, which is 36. The product is 120960.

57
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:04,956
The answer is the sum: 182586. A computer expansion of the series gives the same number.

58
00:10:05,406 --> 00:10:16,505
Common mistakes: forgetting the factor 4 to the j, reading x to the 70 and not x to the 67, and using the wrong top number in the binomial coefficient.

59
00:10:16,955 --> 00:10:24,014
Question 8. A of x is the sum of x to the n over n factorial. Pause the video and try all three parts.

60
00:10:24,464 --> 00:10:32,591
Part a. Put 2 x in place of x. 2 x to the n equals 2 to the n times x to the n.

61
00:10:33,041 --> 00:10:45,719
So the coefficient in position n is multiplied by 2 to the n. The row 1, 1, one half, one sixth becomes 1, 2, 2, four thirds.

62
00:10:46,169 --> 00:10:51,881
The answer to part a: the coefficient of x to the n in A of 2 x is 2 to the n over n factorial.

63
00:10:52,331 --> 00:11:02,017
Part b. Use the product formula. The coefficient of x to the n in A times B is the sum over k of a sub k times b sub n minus k.

64
00:11:02,467 --> 00:11:15,563
Draw the grid. Row i is the term of degree i in the first factor. Column j is the term of degree j in the second. The cell holds the product of the two coefficients, one over i factorial j factorial.

65
00:11:16,013 --> 00:11:23,391
The cells with i plus j equal to n give the terms of x to the n. For n equal to 3, this is the purple diagonal.

66
00:11:23,841 --> 00:11:35,590
The sum is one sixth plus one half plus one half plus one sixth. That is four thirds. It equals 2 cubed over 3 factorial. This agrees with part a.

67
00:11:36,040 --> 00:11:49,415
In general, the coefficient of x to the n in A of x squared is the sum over k from 0 to n of one over k factorial times n minus k factorial. This is a finite sum, as the question asks.

68
00:11:49,865 --> 00:12:06,305
Part c. Prove that A of 2 x equals A of x squared. Compare the coefficients. Line 1: by part b, the coefficient of x to the n in A of x squared is the sum of one over k factorial times n minus k factorial.

69
00:12:06,755 --> 00:12:16,228
Line 2: multiply and divide by n factorial. Each term becomes n choose k. So we have one over n factorial, times the sum of n choose k.

70
00:12:16,678 --> 00:12:31,028
Line 3: by the Binomial Theorem with x equal to 1, the sum of n choose k over k is 2 to the n. The picture shows it: each row of binomial coefficients sums to a power of 2. Row 3 is 1, 3, 3, 1, with sum 8.

71
00:12:31,478 --> 00:12:37,237
Line 4: by part a, 2 to the n over n factorial is the coefficient of x to the n in A of 2 x.

72
00:12:37,687 --> 00:12:52,733
This holds for every n in N. Two formal power series are equal when all their coefficients are equal. So A of 2 x equals A of x squared. Name the results you use: the product formula and the Binomial Theorem.

73
00:12:53,183 --> 00:13:07,023
Question 9. P is the set of palindromes: the strings that do not change when we reverse them. Find an unambiguous expression, the generating series by length, and a reason why the expression is unambiguous. Pause the video and try it.

74
00:13:07,473 --> 00:13:16,343
Take the palindrome 0110110. Its first bit equals its last bit. Both are 0.

75
00:13:16,793 --> 00:13:23,890
Remove the two end bits. The middle, 11011, is again a palindrome. Its end bits are both 1.

76
00:13:24,340 --> 00:13:28,241
Remove them. 101 remains. It is a palindrome too.

77
00:13:28,691 --> 00:13:35,286
Remove the ends again. The single bit 0 remains. A string of length 0 or 1 is a palindrome.

78
00:13:35,736 --> 00:13:48,785
This gives a recursive expression. P is epsilon, or 0, or 1, or 0 P 0, or 1 P 1. The notes use the same idea for the set B equal to epsilon or 0 B 1.

79
00:13:49,235 --> 00:13:52,393
Now show that each palindrome is produced exactly once.

80
00:13:52,843 --> 00:14:11,167
A string of length 0 is produced only by epsilon. A string of length 1 is produced only by 0 or by 1. A string of length at least 2 must come from 0 P 0 or 1 P 1. Its first bit equals its last bit, because f of s equals s.

81
00:14:11,617 --> 00:14:17,004
The first bit tells us which case. A string that starts with 0 can only come from 0 P 0.

82
00:14:17,454 --> 00:14:30,312
Then the middle is a shorter palindrome. By induction on the length, the middle is produced exactly once. So the whole string is produced exactly once. Also each string produced is a palindrome, so the expression gives exactly P.

83
00:14:30,762 --> 00:14:42,093
The expression is unambiguous, so the Sum and Product Lemmas give the equation. Phi of x equals 1, plus x, plus x, plus x squared phi, plus x squared phi.

84
00:14:42,543 --> 00:14:48,301
Solve for phi: phi of x equals 1 plus 2 x, over 1 minus 2 x squared.

85
00:14:48,751 --> 00:15:03,101
Check. The coefficients are 1, 2, 2, 4, 4, 8, 8. A palindrome is fixed by its first half, so there are 2 to the ceiling of n over 2 of length n. A computer count up to length 14 agrees.

86
00:15:03,551 --> 00:15:17,065
Question 10 a. Solve the recurrence a sub n minus 7 a sub n minus 1 plus 6 a sub n minus 2 equals 0, with a sub 0 equal to 5 and a sub 1 equal to 10. Pause the video and try it.

87
00:15:17,515 --> 00:15:29,265
By Theorem 4.8, the generating series is P of x over Q of x, with Q of x equal to 1 minus 7 x plus 6 x squared. The coefficients of Q are the coefficients of the recurrence.

88
00:15:29,715 --> 00:15:42,207
Find the inverse roots. The auxiliary polynomial is t squared minus 7 t plus 6. It factors as t minus 1 times t minus 6.

89
00:15:42,657 --> 00:15:52,688
So the inverse roots are 1 and 6, each with multiplicity 1. By the Main Theorem, a sub n equals A times 1 to the n plus B times 6 to the n.

90
00:15:53,138 --> 00:16:07,952
For which n does this hold? Theorem 4.8 gives P of x equal to 5 minus 25 x. Its degree is less than 2. So there is no polynomial part R, and the form holds for all n at least 0.

91
00:16:08,402 --> 00:16:23,960
Use the initial conditions. n equal to 0 gives A plus B equal to 5. n equal to 1 gives A plus 6 B equal to 10. Subtract: 5 B equals 5. So B equals 1, and A equals 4.

92
00:16:24,410 --> 00:16:41,593
The answer is a sub n equals 4 plus 6 to the n. The table checks it. The recurrence gives 5, 10, 40, 220, 1300. The formula gives the same numbers. A computer check agrees for the first 20 terms.

93
00:16:42,043 --> 00:16:52,538
You can also check with partial fractions. 4 over one minus x plus 1 over one minus 6 x gives 5 minus 25 x over the product. This is P over Q.

94
00:16:52,988 --> 00:17:01,440
A common mistake is a sign error in Q of x. Write the recurrence with all terms on the left side equal to 0. Then copy its coefficients into Q.

95
00:17:01,890 --> 00:17:08,717
Question 10 b. Here we go the other way: from a formula to a recurrence. Pause the video and try it.

96
00:17:09,167 --> 00:17:14,786
The term 3 to the n has a constant coefficient. So 3 is an inverse root with multiplicity 1.

97
00:17:15,236 --> 00:17:25,035
The term n minus 4 times minus 2 to the n has a polynomial of degree 1. So minus 2 is an inverse root with multiplicity 2.

98
00:17:25,485 --> 00:17:35,980
So Q of x is one minus 3 x, times the square of one plus 2 x. Expand it: 1 plus x minus 8 x squared minus 12 x cubed.

99
00:17:36,430 --> 00:18:00,300
To justify this, write the generating series. 3 to the n gives one over one minus 3 x. n times minus 2 to the n gives minus 2 x over the square of one plus 2 x. Minus 4 times minus 2 to the n gives minus 4 over one plus 2 x. Over the common denominator Q, the numerator P has degree at most 2.

100
00:18:00,750 --> 00:18:12,825
By Theorem 4.8, the coefficients of Q give the recurrence. b sub n plus b sub n minus 1, minus 8 b sub n minus 2, minus 12 b sub n minus 3, equals 0.

101
00:18:13,275 --> 00:18:26,649
The numerator has degree at most 2. So the recurrence holds for n at least 3. We need three initial conditions. From the formula, b sub 0 is minus 3, b sub 1 is 9, and b sub 2 is 1.

102
00:18:27,099 --> 00:18:42,908
Check it. For n equal to 3: 35 plus 1, minus 8 times 9, minus 12 times minus 3, gives 0. The rows for n equal to 4 and 5 also give 0. A computer check agrees up to n equal to 19.

103
00:18:43,358 --> 00:18:55,851
A common mistake is a recurrence with too few terms. The order must be the sum of the multiplicities, 1 plus 2, which is 3. A computer check shows that no recurrence of order 2 fits the values.

104
00:18:56,301 --> 00:19:11,672
Here is a summary of question 6. To show an expression is ambiguous, give one string with two productions. For a series, use the Sum, String and Product Lemmas. For a set of strings with a block condition, restrict the block decomposition.

105
00:19:12,122 --> 00:19:21,303
Question 7 used two binomial series and a list of index pairs. Question 8 compared coefficients, with the product formula and the Binomial Theorem.

106
00:19:21,753 --> 00:19:29,183
Question 9 used a recursive expression. Question 10 used Theorem 4.8 and the Main Theorem, in both directions.
